WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.965
Self-hosting versus an API: the cost math.

2
00:00:03.245 --> 00:00:04.675
Now put prices on it.

3
00:00:04.955 --> 00:00:11.568
An API charges per token, so its cost scales linearly with volume: cost per task times tasks.

4
00:00:11.848 --> 00:00:22.301
A rented GPU charges per hour, so its monthly cost is flat as long as one GPU can keep up, then steps up by one GPU’s cost each time you need another.

5
00:00:22.581 --> 00:00:27.189
Hugging Face lists one NVIDIA L4 on AWS at $0.

6
00:00:27.469 --> 00:00:31.267
80 per hour and one A100 80GB at $2.

7
00:00:31.547 --> 00:00:38.053
50 per hour at the time of writing; an always-on GPU runs about 730 hours a month.

8
00:00:38.333 --> 00:00:40.147
Two facts give you the whole picture.

9
00:00:40.427 --> 00:00:54.677
First, the GPU’s best-case cost per task is its hourly price divided by the tasks it completes per hour at the highest utilization you would plan for (planning for 100 percent leaves no headroom for traffic spikes).

10
00:00:54.957 --> 00:01:01.251
If that number is above the API’s cost per task, self-hosting never wins on cost, at any volume.

11
00:01:01.531 --> 00:01:14.544
Second, if it is below, the break-even volume is the monthly GPU cost divided by the API’s cost per task: below that volume you are paying for an idle GPU, above it you are saving money.

12
00:01:14.824 --> 00:01:18.195
Run the numbers with real prices and a pattern appears.

13
00:01:18.475 --> 00:01:27.776
Against a frontier model with a long few-shot prompt, a small fine-tuned model on one GPU breaks even at a few hundred thousand tasks a month.

14
00:01:28.056 --> 00:01:31.661
Against a small, cheap API model, it may never break even.

15
00:01:31.941 --> 00:01:33.883
Anthropic lists Claude Haiku 5.

16
00:01:34.163 --> 00:01:35.528
5 at $0.

17
00:01:35.808 --> 00:01:38.624
10 per million input tokens and $0.

18
00:01:38.904 --> 00:01:46.264
50 per million output tokens at the review date, so a 600-token extraction costs a few hundredths of a cent.

19
00:01:46.544 --> 00:01:56.251
That is why model selection in this program always compares against the cheapest API model that passes your benchmark, not only against the most capable one.

20
00:01:56.531 --> 00:01:59.283
Cost is rarely the only reason to self-host.

21
00:01:59.563 --> 00:02:11.168
Data that can’t leave your network, latency targets an API can’t meet, independence from a provider’s deprecation schedule, or a model no API offers are all legitimate reasons.

22
00:02:11.448 --> 00:02:15.651
Name the reason in your write-up, so cost isn’t doing work it can’t support.
